WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.980
- For better or worse, I get
the McDreamy thing a lot.

00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:05.440
- [Woman] Move over, McDreamy.

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:07.210
- I've never seen the man in action.

00:00:07.210 --> 00:00:10.330
In fact, I've never seen a
full Grey's Anatomy episode.

00:00:10.330 --> 00:00:12.182
But you asked for it, so you got it.

00:00:12.182 --> 00:00:14.932
(relaxing music)

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:18.420
- [Richard] A month ago,
you were in med school

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:19.977
being taught by doctors.

00:00:19.977 --> 00:00:21.550
("Portions for Foxes" by Rilo Kiley)

00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:25.360
Today, you are the doctors.

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:28.270
- First of all, I've never heard a speech

00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:31.680
given that dramatically
on an intern's first day.

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:33.350
Nobody really talks like that.

00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:34.870
It's much more practical.

00:00:34.870 --> 00:00:36.790
- [Richard] Five of you will
crack under the pressure.

00:00:36.790 --> 00:00:39.120
Two of you will be asked to leave.

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:40.940
This is your starting line.

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:42.230
This is your arena.

00:00:42.230 --> 00:00:43.790
- This sounds like Hunger Games

00:00:43.790 --> 00:00:45.543
more than it does Grey's Anatomy.

00:00:45.543 --> 00:00:48.470
("Portions for Foxes" by Rilo Kiley)

00:00:48.470 --> 00:00:50.610
- No, I'm not gay.
- Uh huh.

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:52.640
- It's just that, you know, you were,

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:54.920
I mean, you were very unforgettable and--

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:58.130
- [Man] O'Malley, Yang, Grey, Stevens.

00:00:58.130 --> 00:00:59.870
- We do get ready
together in the mornings.

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:01.430
There are locker rooms like this.

00:01:01.430 --> 00:01:04.450
It's funny to say, but because doctors

00:01:04.450 --> 00:01:08.620
spend a lot of time in books
while in medical school,

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:12.010
they're not the greatest
at interacting with others

00:01:12.010 --> 00:01:14.530
or maybe even showing
interest in the opposite sex.

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:17.570
So I've seen situations
like this happen a lot,

00:01:17.570 --> 00:01:19.290
where someone's trying
to make conversation,

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:22.530
but they're not great at it,
so it comes off very awkward.

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:24.680
It's funny, but this is very accurate.

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:28.000
- Your first shift starts
now and lasts 48 hours.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:30.380
You're interns, grunts, nobodies,

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:32.020
bottom of the surgical food chain.

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:34.550
- Residents don't work
48-hour calls anymore.

00:01:34.550 --> 00:01:36.970
They're capped at 80 hours per week.

00:01:36.970 --> 00:01:39.290
Maybe they go a little
bit over that sometimes.

00:01:39.290 --> 00:01:41.250
But 48 hours is just nonexistent.

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:44.390
And this started because
of the Libby Zion Law,

00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:47.040
which is sadly the name of a young girl

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:50.860
who died because residents
who were so overworked,

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:54.310
they missed catching a diagnosis,
and she ultimately died.

00:01:54.310 --> 00:01:56.840
- You run labs, write orders,
work every second night

00:01:56.840 --> 00:01:58.730
until you drop, and don't complain.

00:01:58.730 --> 00:02:00.400
On-call rooms.

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:01.515
Attendings hog them.
(Mike laughs)

00:02:01.515 --> 00:02:02.740
Sleep when you can where you--

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:05.150
- The on-call rooms
look exactly like this.

00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:06.240
They're very plain.

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:07.510
They're very basic.

00:02:07.510 --> 00:02:08.970
The hospital doesn't budget or

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:10.540
allocate any money towards them.

00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:12.100
You have to change your own sheets.

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:15.370
The mattresses are basically
plastic, uncomfortable.

00:02:15.370 --> 00:02:16.750
So this is very accurate.

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:18.820
- Dr. Bailey, let's shotgun her.

00:02:18.820 --> 00:02:20.280
- That means every test in the book,

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:22.890
CT, CBC, CHEM-7, tox screen.

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:25.330
- I love how simply the attending

00:02:25.330 --> 00:02:26.900
came in and made that decision.

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:30.280
He basically saw maybe the chart,

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:32.070
maybe a brief explanation of what happened

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:34.040
while the patient came in, and he already

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:35.750
has a shotgun approach to this?

00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:37.200
This is not how medicine works.

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:38.290
That's ineffective.

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:39.970
You need to have a presumed diagnosis

00:02:39.970 --> 00:02:42.360
in place so that the labs and tests

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:45.310
you order actually confirm a diagnosis.

00:02:45.310 --> 00:02:47.090
You can't just throw
stuff against the wall,

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:49.000
shotgun against the
wall, whatever he said,

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:50.190
and hope something sticks.

00:02:50.190 --> 00:02:51.760
The only time that is appropriate

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:54.070
is if you have no idea why a young,

00:02:54.070 --> 00:02:57.020
otherwise healthy patient
with an unknown history

00:02:57.020 --> 00:02:59.440
comes in with some sort
of weird presentation.

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:01.940
Then you can start ordering
a broad range of tests.

00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:04.940
But they knew she had a seizure history,

00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:07.180
so why are they doing a CT scan?

00:03:07.180 --> 00:03:08.220
I need more info.

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:09.556
More info, please.

00:03:09.556 --> 00:03:10.389
- Hello?

00:03:11.420 --> 00:03:12.253
You're so lost.

00:03:12.253 --> 00:03:13.380
What are you, like, new?

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:15.880
- This is where these
medical dramas get me.

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:18.550
They make it seem like
the doctors do everything.

00:03:18.550 --> 00:03:21.090
From A to Z, they diagnose the patient,

00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:22.420
they run the tests on the patient,

00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:24.860
they draw the blood, they
transport the patient.

00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:27.830
We have nurses, we have MAs,
we have transport staff,

00:03:27.830 --> 00:03:31.830
we have PT therapists, we
have respiratory therapists.

00:03:31.830 --> 00:03:32.870
I understand that they're trying

00:03:32.870 --> 00:03:34.290
to build the character, so they're trying

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:35.780
to show as much of them as possible.

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:39.440
- I'm just gonna insert my
fingers into your rectum.

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:41.400
- Why fingers?

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:42.233
Just one finger.

00:03:42.233 --> 00:03:43.955
- Bet you missed a lot
when you first started out.

00:03:43.955 --> 00:03:46.380
("They" by JEM)

00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:47.570
- No one was ever that mean.

00:03:47.570 --> 00:03:48.810
People wanted you to learn.

00:03:48.810 --> 00:03:50.150
They wanted you to get better.

00:03:50.150 --> 00:03:53.690
If you go into every
procedure or every moment

00:03:53.690 --> 00:03:55.730
within residency with the concept

00:03:55.730 --> 00:03:57.650
that you wanna learn
and you wanna do better,

00:03:57.650 --> 00:04:01.540
people respect that, so I
don't necessarily get this

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:03.899
hatred that these poor
interns are getting.

00:04:03.899 --> 00:04:06.560
(people talking indistinctly)

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:09.220
Our cafeterias are bare bones

00:04:09.220 --> 00:04:11.750
with usually not the
greatest food available.

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:14.520
In fact, this is what surprises
me most about hospitals.

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:16.910
We try and tell people to
eat healthy and stay healthy,

00:04:16.910 --> 00:04:20.210
but the only options to
eat are pizza, burgers,

00:04:20.210 --> 00:04:22.960
chicken fingers, the most
unhealthy foods possible.

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:23.850
It's crazy to me.

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:26.320
- You know what, I'm not the doctor.

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:29.290
I'm a doctor, but I'm not Katie's doctor,

00:04:29.290 --> 00:04:30.870
so I'll go get him for you.

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:33.250
- Very common situation
to find yourself in,

00:04:33.250 --> 00:04:36.350
especially on nights when you're covering

00:04:36.350 --> 00:04:40.030
for the entire hospital and
some medical emergency happens.

00:04:40.030 --> 00:04:42.540
The family wants answers,
they have a lot of very

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:44.270
important questions
that they need answered,

00:04:44.270 --> 00:04:45.760
but you don't know the patient.

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:47.660
You need to put a lot
of information together

00:04:47.660 --> 00:04:50.910
rather quickly and
create a thought process

00:04:50.910 --> 00:04:53.030
that the family can follow along with you

00:04:53.030 --> 00:04:54.490
so they know what's going on.

00:04:54.490 --> 00:04:56.940
You don't have to give a direct answer.

00:04:56.940 --> 00:04:58.900
A lot of residents, young
residents especially,

00:04:58.900 --> 00:05:00.440
think that if they don't have the exact,

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:03.220
right, truthful answer that they're bad.

00:05:03.220 --> 00:05:04.053
That's not true.

00:05:04.053 --> 00:05:07.310
Even seasoned attendings know
that there's times to say,

00:05:07.310 --> 00:05:08.910
we don't know yet what's going on.

00:05:08.910 --> 00:05:10.510
- Dr. Shepherd, he's over there.

00:05:11.893 --> 00:05:14.370
(playful music)
(Mike laughs)

00:05:14.370 --> 00:05:18.170
- Why did I know this McDreamy
guy is gonna pop into scene?

00:05:18.170 --> 00:05:20.520
Oh wait, did they not know each other?

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:23.772
- Dr. Shepherd, this is inappropriate.

00:05:23.772 --> 00:05:24.980
(playful music)

00:05:24.980 --> 00:05:26.180
Has that ever occurred to you?

00:05:26.180 --> 00:05:27.180
- I agree with her.

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:30.030
In a position of power, you should not

00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:32.410
be involved in these types of situations.

00:05:32.410 --> 00:05:33.620
They don't happen that often.

00:05:33.620 --> 00:05:35.240
As much as we like to believe that there's

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:37.560
so much love and drama
going on in hospitals,

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:39.260
it doesn't happen as much
as you think it does.

00:05:39.260 --> 00:05:43.590
But I have a little, she
may come back for more

00:05:43.590 --> 00:05:45.761
with the wonderful Dr. Shepherd.

00:05:45.761 --> 00:05:46.790
("Dance" by O.A.O.T.S)

00:05:46.790 --> 00:05:48.232
- [Preston] Max him out.

00:05:48.232 --> 00:05:50.050
♪ Innocent ♪

00:05:50.050 --> 00:05:53.410
- Usually we wear eye protection
'cause if blood squirts out

00:05:53.410 --> 00:05:55.480
into your eye, it's pretty dangerous.

00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:57.990
So at least have goggles
or the face shield

00:05:57.990 --> 00:05:59.534
that comes attached to the mask.

00:05:59.534 --> 00:06:00.517
("Dance" by O.A.O.T.S)

00:06:00.517 --> 00:06:02.800
(interns cheer)

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:03.656
- [George] Appendix is out.

00:06:03.656 --> 00:06:05.746
- Oh!
(Meredith laughs)

00:06:05.746 --> 00:06:06.579
- [Preston] Not bad.

00:06:06.579 --> 00:06:07.600
- That was rather quick.

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:08.970
I guess that's for TV's sake.

00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:10.860
In reality, there's a lot of conversation

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:13.430
that occurs between the
attending and the intern,

00:06:13.430 --> 00:06:16.310
especially with identifying landmarks.

00:06:16.310 --> 00:06:17.580
They'll often ask you really

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:19.970
hard questions, and we call that pimping.

00:06:19.970 --> 00:06:21.690
And they'll ask you questions

00:06:21.690 --> 00:06:23.130
that are unrelated to the case.

00:06:23.130 --> 00:06:24.780
So, you prepare for this case,

00:06:24.780 --> 00:06:25.973
but they'll ask you something else.

00:06:25.973 --> 00:06:27.200
It's very common.

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:28.350
- [Preston] Bokhee, give him a clamp.

00:06:28.350 --> 00:06:29.930
- [Bokhee] BP's dropping.

00:06:29.930 --> 00:06:30.980
- He's choking.

00:06:30.980 --> 00:06:32.040
- Come on, George.

00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:33.006
- [Preston] Today.

00:06:33.006 --> 00:06:33.839
- This is so dramatic.
- Pull your balls

00:06:33.839 --> 00:06:35.390
out of your back pocket, let's go.

00:06:35.390 --> 00:06:36.550
- Who talks like that?

00:06:36.550 --> 00:06:38.547
Pull your balls out of your back pocket.

00:06:38.547 --> 00:06:40.070
- 007 is a state of mind.

00:06:40.070 --> 00:06:43.226
- Says the girl who finished
first in her class at Stanford.

00:06:43.226 --> 00:06:45.150
(pager beeps)

00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:47.130
- We lived with the vending machines.

00:06:47.130 --> 00:06:50.230
I used to eat Snickers
galore when I forgot

00:06:50.230 --> 00:06:52.220
to bring my healthy
alternatives from home.

00:06:52.220 --> 00:06:54.910
- Mr. Jones has junky veins and
he really needs antibiotics.

00:06:54.910 --> 00:06:56.610
I should start a central line.

00:06:56.610 --> 00:06:57.443
- So start one.

00:06:58.760 --> 00:07:00.060
You don't know how.

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:01.664
- I, (sighs)

00:07:01.664 --> 00:07:04.730
(coughs) clearly I'm very
passionate about this.

00:07:04.730 --> 00:07:07.820
Starting a central line just
because you couldn't get

00:07:07.820 --> 00:07:12.030
a vein with a needle prick is crazy.

00:07:12.030 --> 00:07:13.560
It's crazy.

00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:15.420
You're putting the patient at risk.

00:07:15.420 --> 00:07:17.005
- [Izzie] Dr. Bailey, I don't
mean to bother you, but--

00:07:17.005 --> 00:07:17.930
- Then don't.

00:07:17.930 --> 00:07:19.000
- [Izzie] It's Mr. Jones.

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:20.310
- Is he dying?

00:07:20.310 --> 00:07:21.143
- No.

00:07:21.143 --> 00:07:22.640
- [Miranda] Then stop talking to me.

00:07:22.640 --> 00:07:24.350
- She should've gone to a nurse

00:07:24.350 --> 00:07:26.240
because nurses have way more experience

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:29.330
than even doctors in getting a blood draw.

00:07:29.330 --> 00:07:32.327
- Next time you wake me, he
better be so close to dead

00:07:32.327 --> 00:07:33.210
there's a tag--
- And that patient

00:07:33.210 --> 00:07:36.470
doesn't look alive for some reason.

00:07:36.470 --> 00:07:37.320
Oh, no, he blinked.

00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:38.287
Okay, he's alive.

00:07:38.287 --> 00:07:41.100
- Are you sure that's the right diagnosis?

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:42.500
- I don't know, I'm only an intern.

00:07:42.500 --> 00:07:43.980
Here's an idea, why don't you go spend

00:07:43.980 --> 00:07:45.329
four years in med school and then

00:07:45.329 --> 00:07:47.180
let me know if it's the right diagnosis?

00:07:47.180 --> 00:07:51.540
- Ooh, that's what we
call a cowboy intern.

00:07:51.540 --> 00:07:53.420
Basically a cowboy intern is someone

00:07:53.420 --> 00:07:56.300
who is so confident in their diagnosis

00:07:56.300 --> 00:07:58.280
that they start their treatment,

00:07:58.280 --> 00:07:59.930
they don't explore any other options,

00:07:59.930 --> 00:08:01.330
they don't have a good differential,

00:08:01.330 --> 00:08:04.460
meaning other options of
what the illness could be.

00:08:04.460 --> 00:08:07.310
Nurses keep the hospital running.

00:08:07.310 --> 00:08:09.860
Without them, we would not
be able to do our jobs.

00:08:09.860 --> 00:08:11.780
A lot of times they'll catch our mistakes.

00:08:11.780 --> 00:08:15.020
They'll be able to identify
things we could be doing better.

00:08:15.020 --> 00:08:17.110
This is the most drama
I've ever seen happen

00:08:17.110 --> 00:08:19.858
in the first 24 hours of being an intern.

00:08:19.858 --> 00:08:21.210
- [Mrs. Bryce] What took you so long?

00:08:21.210 --> 00:08:22.470
- [Christian] Lorazepam's not working.

00:08:22.470 --> 00:08:24.200
- Phenobarbital, load
her with phenobarbital.

00:08:24.200 --> 00:08:25.640
- Okay, when you say load her

00:08:25.640 --> 00:08:28.350
with phenobarbital, that's not an order.

00:08:28.350 --> 00:08:30.360
You need to give the dosage.

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:32.410
The nurse needs to say back to you

00:08:32.410 --> 00:08:34.730
to make sure that she heard
the same thing that you said.

00:08:34.730 --> 00:08:37.430
Otherwise, if the nurse misheard you

00:08:37.430 --> 00:08:38.960
and you didn't even give the instruction

00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:41.110
on how much to give,
they can give any dose,

00:08:41.110 --> 00:08:42.530
and that can be lethal to a patient.

00:08:42.530 --> 00:08:43.371
So that's not real.

00:08:43.371 --> 00:08:44.395
(heart monitor flatlining)
- Heart stopping.

00:08:44.395 --> 00:08:45.890
- [Nurse] Code blue, code blue!

00:08:45.890 --> 00:08:48.510
- Okay, so heart stopping,
that's the code blue.

00:08:48.510 --> 00:08:49.940
I've talked about this before.

00:08:49.940 --> 00:08:51.530
Now the patient is dead.

00:08:51.530 --> 00:08:54.160
If the heart has stopped, the
patient is technically dead,

00:08:54.160 --> 00:08:56.040
and you have to work to revive them

00:08:56.040 --> 00:08:57.690
to get their heart started back up again.

00:08:57.690 --> 00:08:59.210
As the resident in charge, you have

00:08:59.210 --> 00:09:01.580
to instantly give roles to everybody.

00:09:01.580 --> 00:09:03.610
You do chest compressions, you do this,

00:09:03.610 --> 00:09:06.840
you give the medications,
you monitor the rhythm strip,

00:09:06.840 --> 00:09:09.600
the most important role
being chest compressions.

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:11.650
You have to do chest compressions.

00:09:11.650 --> 00:09:14.130
You cannot stop doing chest compressions.

00:09:14.130 --> 00:09:16.090
So, if you have one person that's giving

00:09:16.090 --> 00:09:17.860
bad chest compressions
'cause they're tired,

00:09:17.860 --> 00:09:19.700
you instantly have to swap them out

00:09:19.700 --> 00:09:22.550
because chest compressions is
what extends and saves lives.

00:09:22.550 --> 00:09:25.380
So right now, them not doing
chest compressions is awful.

00:09:25.380 --> 00:09:27.149
The first thing you do
is chest compressions.

00:09:27.149 --> 00:09:27.982
(heart monitor flatlining)

00:09:27.982 --> 00:09:29.950
- [Nurse] Wait.

00:09:29.950 --> 00:09:31.560
- Charge the paddles.
- Whoa.

00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:33.880
I hope she's not gonna shock the patient.

00:09:33.880 --> 00:09:34.950
Oh my god, please tell me--
- Plus 200.

00:09:34.950 --> 00:09:35.900
- She's not gonna.
- Charge.

00:09:35.900 --> 00:09:36.733
- No.

00:09:36.733 --> 00:09:38.060
- Clear.
- No, what are you doing,

00:09:38.060 --> 00:09:39.730
Grey's Anatomy?
(machine beeps)

00:09:39.730 --> 00:09:41.354
No!
- Still V-fib, nothing.

00:09:41.354 --> 00:09:43.333
- No, oh, she's in V-fib?
- 19 seconds.

00:09:43.333 --> 00:09:44.340
- Charge to 300.
- Then why does it sound

00:09:44.340 --> 00:09:45.632
like she's flatlining?

00:09:45.632 --> 00:09:47.610
♪ Ground where you lay ♪

00:09:47.610 --> 00:09:49.553
♪ You've been laying there so long ♪

00:09:49.553 --> 00:09:51.800
- Ooh, I've never thrown up in a hospital.

00:09:51.800 --> 00:09:53.480
I've never seen a doctor throw up

00:09:53.480 --> 00:09:54.870
in the hospital, unless they were sick.

00:09:54.870 --> 00:09:56.540
Yeah, when things go
wrong, you get nauseous.

00:09:56.540 --> 00:09:57.690
That's part of life.

00:09:57.690 --> 00:10:00.260
I mean, there's difficult
moments that happen.

00:10:00.260 --> 00:10:02.740
There's times where
you're gonna be humbled.

00:10:02.740 --> 00:10:04.370
I mean, every day I walked into the

00:10:04.370 --> 00:10:06.050
hospital as a resident, I was humbled.

00:10:06.050 --> 00:10:10.600
There's a reason why we
call it practicing medicine.

00:10:10.600 --> 00:10:13.160
Because we're all practicing,
we're all learning,

00:10:13.160 --> 00:10:15.750
we're all adapting, we're
all trying to get better,

00:10:15.750 --> 00:10:19.180
and the human body's a
crazy, crazy thing that

00:10:19.180 --> 00:10:20.970
we still don't have perfect knowledge of.

00:10:20.970 --> 00:10:23.680
So I feel for her a little
bit, just a little bit.

00:10:23.680 --> 00:10:26.160
A little dramatic, but I feel for her.

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:26.993
There's our man.
- You said it was

00:10:26.993 --> 00:10:28.460
a seizure disorder.

00:10:28.460 --> 00:10:29.440
Now you're saying it isn't?

00:10:29.440 --> 00:10:31.114
- I'm saying that I don't know.

00:10:31.114 --> 00:10:31.947
- [Mrs. Bryce] Well, what do you--

00:10:31.947 --> 00:10:32.780
- Oh!

00:10:32.780 --> 00:10:35.130
My man McDreamy's saying my line.

00:10:35.130 --> 00:10:39.580
Real doctors are comfortable
saying I don't know.

00:10:39.580 --> 00:10:42.850
Because in reality, there's
a lot we don't know,

00:10:42.850 --> 00:10:45.170
and when cases get very complex,

00:10:45.170 --> 00:10:47.320
like this young girl
who's otherwise healthy

00:10:47.320 --> 00:10:49.890
is having seizures and
then her heart's stopping,

00:10:49.890 --> 00:10:52.740
it's a very unusual
case, very, very unusual.

00:10:52.740 --> 00:10:54.730
- [Mr. Bryce] I want
someone else, a doctor

00:10:54.730 --> 00:10:55.940
who knows what they're doing.

00:10:55.940 --> 00:10:57.614
You get me someone else,
someone better than you.

00:10:57.614 --> 00:11:00.580
- These types of conversations do happen.

00:11:00.580 --> 00:11:01.810
This is real life.

00:11:01.810 --> 00:11:03.980
People get very, very upset.

00:11:03.980 --> 00:11:06.640
It takes a lot of patience
to deal with these issues.

00:11:06.640 --> 00:11:08.600
I've seen doctors get mad and snap.

00:11:08.600 --> 00:11:10.890
What I do is if I feel like
I'm getting angry as well,

00:11:10.890 --> 00:11:12.440
I step away from the situation,

00:11:12.440 --> 00:11:14.450
let both parties cool
down, and then come back

00:11:14.450 --> 00:11:17.180
and explain calmly what we're working on.

00:11:17.180 --> 00:11:18.750
Because if you just say we don't know

00:11:18.750 --> 00:11:21.920
with no follow-up, it seems
like you're not doing anything.

00:11:21.920 --> 00:11:23.960
But if you say, we don't
know, but here's what

00:11:23.960 --> 00:11:25.410
we think it might be and here's what

00:11:25.410 --> 00:11:27.450
we're working towards,
that's a much better answer.

00:11:27.450 --> 00:11:28.910
- He'll be fine, right?

00:11:28.910 --> 00:11:30.310
- Tony's gonna sail through.

00:11:33.460 --> 00:11:35.640
You have nothing to worry about, promise.

00:11:35.640 --> 00:11:37.130
- Patients need reassurance.

00:11:37.130 --> 00:11:38.640
You need to give honest reassurance

00:11:38.640 --> 00:11:40.260
without over-promising them results.

00:11:40.260 --> 00:11:41.510
- [Meredith] What are you doing?

00:11:41.510 --> 00:11:43.610
- I'm suturing a banana with the vain

00:11:43.610 --> 00:11:45.200
hope that it wakes up my brain.

00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:46.033
(Mike laughs)

00:11:46.033 --> 00:11:48.350
- Suturing bananas, suturing pig's feet

00:11:48.350 --> 00:11:50.180
is something very common that med students

00:11:50.180 --> 00:11:52.810
and interns do, so it is true.

00:11:52.810 --> 00:11:54.440
- She's gonna die if I don't make

00:11:54.440 --> 00:11:56.910
a diagnosis, which is where you come in.

00:11:56.910 --> 00:11:58.640
I can't do it alone.

00:11:58.640 --> 00:12:01.555
I need your extra minds, extra eyes.

00:12:01.555 --> 00:12:03.910
- (chuckles) This is very dramatic.

00:12:03.910 --> 00:12:07.870
Every day, we have
meetings known as rounds

00:12:07.870 --> 00:12:09.460
where we sit around a table like this,

00:12:09.460 --> 00:12:11.260
we present a complicated patient,

00:12:11.260 --> 00:12:13.030
we give the history, we give the physical,

00:12:13.030 --> 00:12:16.320
we give the test results,
and we all brainstorm ideas

00:12:16.320 --> 00:12:19.400
of what it could be, how
we would approach the case.

00:12:19.400 --> 00:12:20.560
And it's very good practice,

00:12:20.560 --> 00:12:22.680
even if we already know the outcome.

00:12:22.680 --> 00:12:24.190
But sometimes we don't know the outcome

00:12:24.190 --> 00:12:25.840
and we are brainstorming
like he said here.

00:12:25.840 --> 00:12:27.190
But it's not this dramatic.

00:12:27.190 --> 00:12:30.206
- You can't comment, make a
face, or react in any way.

00:12:30.206 --> 00:12:32.480
(playful music)

00:12:32.480 --> 00:12:33.343
We had sex.

00:12:35.620 --> 00:12:36.830
- Bum bum bum.

00:12:36.830 --> 00:12:38.340
- Katie competes in beauty pageants.

00:12:38.340 --> 00:12:40.280
- I know that, but we have
to save her life anyway.

00:12:40.280 --> 00:12:42.260
- Okay, she has no
headaches, no neck pain.

00:12:42.260 --> 00:12:43.560
Her CT is clean.

00:12:43.560 --> 00:12:45.570
There's no medical proof of an aneurysm.

00:12:45.570 --> 00:12:49.970
- First of all, this is
known as a HIPAA violation.

00:12:49.970 --> 00:12:52.630
They're basically talking
about a patient's case,

00:12:52.630 --> 00:12:54.610
using her name in front of other people

00:12:54.610 --> 00:12:56.280
who are not involved in her case.

00:12:56.280 --> 00:12:58.530
And that is strictly against the law.

00:12:58.530 --> 00:13:00.330
They could get sued for
millions of dollars,

00:13:00.330 --> 00:13:01.810
and rightfully so, because you shouldn't

00:13:01.810 --> 00:13:04.010
be discussing patient
information in elevators.

00:13:04.010 --> 00:13:06.170
In fact, in most hospitals, you'll see

00:13:06.170 --> 00:13:07.840
a sign in the elevator that says,

00:13:07.840 --> 00:13:09.860
please don't discuss patient information.

00:13:09.860 --> 00:13:10.840
- Wow, that was quick.

00:13:10.840 --> 00:13:13.073
- His heart had too much
damage to give him a bypass.

00:13:13.073 --> 00:13:14.711
("Life Is Short" by Butterfly Boucher)

00:13:14.711 --> 00:13:15.711
I had to let him go.

00:13:16.960 --> 00:13:19.840
It happens rarely, but it does happen.

00:13:19.840 --> 00:13:20.890
The worst part of the game.

00:13:20.890 --> 00:13:23.240
- Ooh, and he gave that
promise to the family

00:13:23.240 --> 00:13:24.950
saying that he's definitely gonna make it.

00:13:24.950 --> 00:13:27.463
- Tony's heart had a lot of damage.

00:13:29.070 --> 00:13:33.260
- You don't do this type of conversation

00:13:33.260 --> 00:13:34.470
in an open setting like this.

00:13:34.470 --> 00:13:37.010
You invite the family
into a counseling room.

00:13:37.010 --> 00:13:38.530
You explain what happened.

00:13:38.530 --> 00:13:40.030
But you don't do it out
in the open like this

00:13:40.030 --> 00:13:41.640
'cause this is just gonna end badly.

00:13:41.640 --> 00:13:43.290
- You shouldn't let the
fact that we had sex

00:13:43.290 --> 00:13:45.870
get in the way of you taking your shot.

00:13:45.870 --> 00:13:49.380
- Shouldn't have had sex
period, Dr. Shepherd.

00:13:49.380 --> 00:13:51.503
- Name the common causes of post-op fever.

00:13:52.370 --> 00:13:54.070
- The five Ws, bruh.

00:13:54.070 --> 00:13:57.513
- Can anybody name the common
causes of post-op fever?

00:13:58.410 --> 00:13:59.620
- I don't like this, says--

00:13:59.620 --> 00:14:03.000
- Wind, water, wound,
walking, wonder drugs.

00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:04.350
- Yeah, she got the five Ws!
- The five Ws.

00:14:04.350 --> 00:14:05.600
- How would you diagnose?

00:14:06.660 --> 00:14:09.200
- I hope she says CT scan--
- Spiral CT.

00:14:09.200 --> 00:14:10.490
- And not a VT scan--
- V/Q scan--

00:14:10.490 --> 00:14:12.120
- Or something.
- Provide O2,

00:14:12.120 --> 00:14:15.078
dose with Heparin, and
consult for an IVC filter.

00:14:15.078 --> 00:14:15.911
- No!

00:14:15.911 --> 00:14:18.950
She just gave all of the
options that you have

00:14:18.950 --> 00:14:23.540
in testing somebody for
a pulmonary embolism,

00:14:23.540 --> 00:14:25.470
as well as treatments that you may

00:14:25.470 --> 00:14:27.990
give to someone with a pulmonary embolism.

00:14:27.990 --> 00:14:28.910
- I'd know you anywhere.

00:14:28.910 --> 00:14:31.740
You're the spitting image of your mother.

00:14:31.740 --> 00:14:33.340
Welcome to the game.

00:14:33.340 --> 00:14:34.440
("Into the Fire" by Thirteen Senses)

00:14:34.440 --> 00:14:35.780
- Welcome to the game.

00:14:35.780 --> 00:14:37.680
I've never heard a doctor speak like that.

00:14:37.680 --> 00:14:39.688
Welcome to the game.

00:14:39.688 --> 00:14:42.480
♪ Pull up from one extreme ♪

00:14:42.480 --> 00:14:44.350
- You never wanna sleep on a stretcher

00:14:44.350 --> 00:14:48.570
as a resident because it's bad luck.

00:14:48.570 --> 00:14:51.150
It basically says that you're
gonna wind up on a stretcher.

00:14:51.150 --> 00:14:52.770
You have the on-call rooms.

00:14:52.770 --> 00:14:54.610
You have couches you can knock out on.

00:14:54.610 --> 00:14:57.030
So sleeping on a stretcher,
that's just bad form.

00:14:57.030 --> 00:14:58.783
- I should go do this.

00:14:58.783 --> 00:15:01.550
(gentle music)

00:15:01.550 --> 00:15:02.570
- You should.

00:15:02.570 --> 00:15:03.863
- The romance is real.

00:15:06.770 --> 00:15:07.870
- I'll see you around.

00:15:09.167 --> 00:15:10.890
- I thought McDreamy would have
a little more muscle on him.

00:15:10.890 --> 00:15:12.869
He kinda is looking a little frail there.

00:15:12.869 --> 00:15:14.380
(upbeat music)

00:15:14.380 --> 00:15:15.630
Boom, there you have it.

00:15:15.630 --> 00:15:19.000
My first Grey's Anatomy
episode is in the books,

00:15:19.000 --> 00:15:21.220
and you were here to
experience it with me.

00:15:21.220 --> 00:15:23.530
All in all, I'm pleasantly surprised

00:15:23.530 --> 00:15:25.200
with this Grey's Anatomy episode

00:15:25.200 --> 00:15:27.210
because I assumed it was gonna be just

00:15:27.210 --> 00:15:29.570
way more loving and sex and that's what

00:15:29.570 --> 00:15:30.850
the whole show was gonna be about.

00:15:30.850 --> 00:15:33.550
And in reality, it did take a deep dive

00:15:33.550 --> 00:15:37.470
into how a resident feels
and what their work is like.

00:15:37.470 --> 00:15:39.800
So I thought that was pretty
good, and it surprised me.

00:15:39.800 --> 00:15:42.250
Medical accuracy obviously weak.

00:15:42.250 --> 00:15:44.370
But I don't think most
people watch Grey's Anatomy

00:15:44.370 --> 00:15:46.660
'cause they wanna become doctors

00:15:46.660 --> 00:15:48.950
and they wanna learn about the human body.

00:15:48.950 --> 00:15:51.920
I think it's an entertainment
show first and foremost,

00:15:51.920 --> 00:15:53.830
and I think it does a great job at that.

00:15:53.830 --> 00:15:56.840
I told you if you got my
Real Doctor vs TV Doctor

00:15:56.840 --> 00:15:59.460
video to 10,000 likes,
I would watch an episode

00:15:59.460 --> 00:16:01.780
of Grey's Anatomy, and I did as promised.

00:16:01.780 --> 00:16:04.640
So, now if you want me
to watch another episode

00:16:04.640 --> 00:16:07.860
of Grey's Anatomy, one of your
favorites, or The Good Doctor

00:16:07.860 --> 00:16:10.580
or House, leave it down
below in the comments.

00:16:10.580 --> 00:16:13.670
Get this video now to 15,000 likes,

00:16:13.670 --> 00:16:15.330
which I know you're totally capable of

00:16:15.330 --> 00:16:18.210
because the last video
you got to 19,000 likes.

00:16:18.210 --> 00:16:19.890
Please subscribe to the channel

00:16:19.890 --> 00:16:21.740
because the more subscribers we have,

00:16:21.740 --> 00:16:25.350
the more content and better
content I can constantly create.

00:16:25.350 --> 00:16:27.897
As always, stay happy and healthy.

00:16:27.897 --> 00:16:30.647
(relaxing music)

